#passage(("You have a universe. The universe has a beginning. The String Theory is about gravity and general relativity. That has nothing to do with what we're talking about. How is the String Theory related to the beginning of the universe? By the way, there is consensus today among physicists that there was a start of the universe. There's 17 different models of how the Big Bang works. All of them agree on one thing, that the universe started from that point. Space and time is what we call the universe. \"Beginning\" is a time question. So, when I say \"Do you agree that the universe has a beginning?\", there's no point in saying \"time and space started\".", "CMBR is left over radiation from the expansion of the BigBang. That's one of the evidences. Another thing which is what we call \"Redshifting\", where you have the wavelength of the Galaxy stretching. We can observe that today with telescopes. The third thing is that you have the Laws of Thermodynamics. Like the second law of thermodynamics: Energy cannot be created or destroyed within a closed system. So, you make the conclusion that the system was closed. There was a point where it started expanding from.", "So, there are multiple pieces of evidence that physicists put to say that the universe has a beginning. They don't just wake up one day and say \"I'm feeling like it, so the universe has a beginning!\" There is evidence for it and there's observable evidence as well, like Redshifting. You can now bring a mega telescope and you can see it with your own eyes. No one has to tell you. It's impossible for the fabric of space to be stretching if the universe is not expanding.", "You have to separate between science as a theory based on induction and between observations. Like when science observes, for example, that the Earth is round, is there any doubt about that? Like water boils at 100°C. There's a scientific experience that took place. That's a fact. That's a reality. So, there are things that are observable, experimented on, repeatable, that we can call facts, that will always be the way they are. Not everything in science is like that.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Questions The Quran! Muhammed Ali - Speakers Corner”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=P_3UFyI5Xm0", ((589, "9:49–11:00"), (694, "11:34–12:00"), (720, "12:00–12:25"), (758, "12:38–13:14"),))
// U ML310:173-247 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("I don't think people previously were thinking there was nothing. It's a baby science. Where we started seeing these particles – there's very many things in creation – when we started seeing them now, how do we interpret the moving and interacting is all of these different interpretations of quantum mechanics. So it's not like that people thought there was nothing and all of a sudden we started thinking there was something. But all of this is still irrelevant to what we're saying. Still the universe exists.", "Why would you call the universe, nothing of it that we observe, is eternal? Everything in the universe has a starting point, has an ending point, and has a cycle that it works in. When you say the universe is eternal, what is it that you're referring to that is always in existence? And the universe would require an explanation out of itself. Because the universe is limited, which is why it's expanding. You can not expand unless you have a limit you can expand from. And the universe is organised in a specific way, which would require by definition an external explanation of itself. So even if you were to accept, or to play with this game, that the universe is eternal, you still would require something else other than the Universe.", "Redshifting is where the fabric of space itself is stretching, which necessitates the universe is expanding. It's irrelevant what you can see, because you can see that the fabric itself is stretching, even if you cannot see way further. Like a carpet: if I can see that the carpet is stretching, I know that the fabric itself is stretching. I know that the universe itself as an entity is stretching. Which scientist says the universe is not expanding because this is the observable universe? Or it could not be expanding because this is just what we can see? These points you're making, none of them are scientific claims; these are just your claims that you made up now. We cannot argue hypotheticals, we argue realities. I'm making a claim about the observable, because we can only talk about the observable.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Unintentionally Proves The Accuracy Of The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1tt_qknxI5I", ((587, "9:47–10:05"), (609, "10:09–10:34"), (670, "11:10–13:14"),))
// U ML298:16-64 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("Where did the universe come from? Big Bang is energy and matter compressed into a singularity that expanded very quickly. Now where did the energy and matter come from? There was no Universe at that time.", "You cannot have an infinite chain of dependent things. Meaning, you cannot have someone that created Allah, because you will ask the same question: who created who created Allah? And then you'll ask who created who created who created Allah? And if we have an infinite chain of dependent things, we wouldn't exist today. But the fact that we exist, it means that there's something Eternal, always existing, that is there. And we say that's what we refer to as \"Allah\". And now can you answer my question about the energy and matter, and how did they expand themselves and where did they come from?", "Even scientists don't have an answer to that question; they're not going to help you there. Because whatever was before the Big Bang is outside the realm of Science, because Science is based on materialism, and it's based on what we can observe. And they say at the time of the Big Bang there was infinite equations, means our laws that we use today are not applicable at that time; therefore we cannot understand what was going on. So they admit that they do not know what was happening at the time with the Big Bang or before it. But we know that energy and matter is not a conscious entity that moves by itself. Atoms do not move by themselves, therefore they would require a cause. First, for them to exist, number one. Number two, for them to expand, for us to have the universe that we have.", "And imagine this: it just happened, and we have this magnificent universe of galaxies and stars and orbits for each planet, electromagnetic waves, Gravity that works precisely allowing Earth to exist. \"Oh! it just happened!\" It just happened to be spot-on. So we're saying which one is a rational conclusion: to claim the \"oh! I don't know, it just happened!\"",), "The Muslim Lantern, “\"Really Smart\" Atheist Encounters A Muslim! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NoDHTVV_RYs", ((32, "0:32–0:56"), (69, "1:09–1:35"), (103, "1:43–2:18"), (144, "2:24–2:41"),))
